(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"Love-And-Duty-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Price of Being the Avatar","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"Aang missed our dinner.\n\nThat sentence should not have mattered as much as it did.\n\nThe city was negotiating control of the eastern rail corridor. Two neighborhoods had threatened to block construction. A council member from the Fire Nation delegation had resigned. Aang had spent the week moving from meeting to meeting with the sort of patience that looked effortless until I watched him come home too tired to finish a bowl of soup.\n\nWe all knew his schedule was impossible.\n\nThe day before the missed dinner, I went to the rail office to find out who had actually asked for Aang.\n\nA clerk handed me a stack of requisitions with the resignation notice on top. Three neighborhood delegates had requested a hearing about whose homes the eastern line would cut through. The council had placed the Avatar's name on the agenda as mediator, witness, and final approving authority. None of the delegates had asked him to approve anything.\n\n“Who put that last job in?” I asked.\n\nThe clerk frowned. “It's what we always do. The Avatar has to keep the peace.”\n\n“Keeping the peace isn't the same as signing away somebody's courtyard.”\n\nShe stiffened. “I didn't write the proposal.”\n\n“I know. I'm asking who did.”\n\nAt home I showed Katara the agenda. She read the addresses, then the names, then the space where the residents' own representative should have been listed.\n\n“They invited the wrong person to speak for them,” she said.\n\n“Again.”\n\nToph took the paper and held it against the table with two fingers. “They could've invited the people who live there. Revolutionary idea.”\n\nThe next day Aang arrived home long after the dinner we had planned. He had spent two hours trying to keep a council member from accusing the delegates of disloyalty. When I told him about the empty representative line, he looked as angry as I'd seen him all week.\n\n“I thought they asked for me,” he said.\n\n“They asked for somebody to listen. You were the name the council wrote down.”\n\nHis eyes closed. “I should've checked.”\n\n“Yeah,” I said. “Next time. Tonight I still wanted dinner with you.”\n\nThat was harder than explaining the paperwork. He looked at the cold bowls, then at me, and didn't try to make what he'd missed disappear with another promise about saving the world.\n\nWe planned dinner anyway.\n\nNot a public appearance.\n\nNot a strategy session.\n\nNot food eaten over maps.\n\nDinner.\n\nKatara cooked. I brought sweet buns. Toph showed up with a bottle of plum wine and claimed this counted as emotional labor.\n\nAang promised to be home by seven.\n\nAt eight, the food was cold.\n\nAt nine, Katara reheated it.\n\nAt ten, Toph opened the wine.\n\nAt eleven, I stopped pretending I was not angry.\n\n“He could have sent a messenger.”\n\nKatara sat across from me with both hands around her cup.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe agreement surprised me.\n\nI had expected her to defend him.\n\nToph stretched out on the floor. “He screwed up.”\n\n“I know he’s busy.”\n\n“That doesn’t make it not a screwup.”\n\nI looked at Katara.\n\nShe seemed tired in a different way than clinic-tired.\n\n“This has happened before,” she said.\n\nThe anger in me changed.\n\n“With you?”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n“Not this exact dinner. But… the Avatar always has somewhere else he could be. Someone else who needs him. Aang hates disappointing people, so sometimes he says yes to everyone until the person he disappoints is the one who already loves him.”\n\nThat hurt because I understood it.\n\nI also hated it.\n\nToph said, “He does it because he knows we won’t start a war if he’s late.”\n\nKatara’s mouth twitched.\n\n“I have considered it.”\n\nThe door opened at eleven thirty.\n\nAang stepped inside looking exhausted and immediately stopped.\n\nThe three of us were still awake.\n\nThe table was still set.\n\nHis face fell.\n\n“Oh.”\n\nToph sat up. “Strong opening.”\n\nAang looked at the clock.\n\nThen at us.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nKatara asked, “What happened?”\n\n“The rail delegation split. Then Minister Shinu arrived early and—”\n\nI stood.\n\nAang stopped.\n\n“I don’t need the reason first,” I said.\n\nHe looked confused.\n\n“I need you to understand what happened here before you explain what happened there.”\n\nSilence.\n\nAang set down his staff.\n\n“You waited.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I should have sent word.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I thought I’d be done in another few minutes.”\n\n“For four hours?”\n\nHe winced.\n\nKatara remained quiet.\n\nToph’s face was unreadable.\n\nAang rubbed both hands over his face. “I’m sorry.”\n\nThe apology sounded sincere.\n\nIt was not enough yet.\n\n“I believe you.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“I’m still angry.”\n\nAang’s shoulders tightened.\n\nThere it was: the instinct to make the feeling go away.\n\nI saw him catch it.\n\nHe breathed.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nThat one word mattered more than another apology would have.\n\nI sat again.\n\nAang took the empty cushion.\n\nKatara finally spoke.\n\n“I need something too.”\n\nHe turned toward her.\n\n“I need you to stop assuming I’ll understand because I always have.”\n\nAang’s face crumpled slightly.\n\n“Katara—”\n\n“No. Listen.”\n\nHe did.\n\n“I know what your work is. I know people can die when you make the wrong choice. I know sometimes you genuinely cannot leave.”\n\nShe leaned forward.\n\n“But I also know you sometimes stay because leaving feels selfish, even when the crisis has enough people without you.”\n\nAang looked down.\n\nToph said, “And you like being needed.”\n\nThe room sharpened.\n\nAang’s head snapped toward her.\n\n“That’s not fair.”\n\nToph did not move.\n\n“Isn’t it?”\n\nHis voice rose. “I don’t do this because I enjoy people depending on me.”\n\n“I didn’t say enjoy.”\n\n“You said I like it.”\n\n“I said being needed feels familiar.”\n\nThat silenced him.\n\nToph’s tone softened, just barely.\n\n“You woke up and the whole world needed you. Maybe sometimes going home feels like abandoning something because nobody’s screaming.”\n\nAang stared at the floor.\n\nKatara’s anger shifted into sadness.\n\nI watched him understand himself in real time.\n\n“That’s awful,” he whispered.\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“It’s a pattern. Not a moral verdict.”\n\nAang laughed once without humor. “You all planned this?”\n\n“No,” Katara said.\n\nToph added, “If we planned it, there’d be snacks.”\n\n“There are sweet buns,” I said.\n\n“Cold sweet buns.”\n\nAang’s mouth twitched.\n\nThe tension broke enough for breathing.\n\nWe made a plan.\n\nIf Aang would be more than thirty minutes late, he sent a messenger unless someone was literally on fire.\n\nIf a meeting had enough competent people without him, he practiced leaving.\n\nIf he needed to stay, he told us rather than disappearing into duty.\n\nAnd if we were angry, he was not allowed to fix the anger by immediately becoming adorable.\n\n“That feels targeted,” he said.\n\n“It is,” Katara replied.\n\nWe ate cold dinner after midnight.\n\nThe food was not good anymore.\n\nThe conversation was.\n\nWhen Toph went to bed, she punched Aang lightly in the shoulder.\n\n“Nice job being emotionally trainable.”\n\n“Thank you?”\n\nKatara kissed his cheek and started clearing dishes.\n\nAang caught her wrist.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“For more than tonight.”\n\nKatara’s face softened.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThey hugged.\n\nI watched without feeling outside it.\n\nThen Aang came to me.\n\nHe stopped close but did not touch.\n\n“Can I?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe wrapped his arms around me.\n\nI let myself be angry and love him at the same time.\n\nThat was another thing I was learning.\n\nLove did not require feelings to arrive one at a time.\n\n“I’m still annoyed,” I murmured into his shoulder.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You have to be up in six hours.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Your orange robe is stained with ink.”\n\n“I—what?”\n\nI pulled back.\n\nKatara started laughing.\n\nAang looked down at the black line across his sleeve.\n\nToph shouted from the hall, “Administrative Companion strikes again!”\n\nAang groaned.\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nThe price of loving the Avatar was that the world would always ask for him.\n\nThe price of being loved by us, apparently, was that sometimes he had to say no.\n\nNeither price was small.\n\nBoth were real.\n\nThe next morning, Aang tried to apologize for being gone by offering to make breakfast.\n\nThis was a tactical error.\n\nHe could cook in the broad sense that food entered a pan and eventually became edible. Katara watched him attack a cabbage with a knife and took the blade away before the meal required healing.\n\n“I wanted to do something for you,” he said.\n\n“You can wash the rice,” she replied.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“Do not involve me. I value my life.”\n\nToph, already eating fruit she had stolen from the counter, said, “I’ll eat whatever survives.”\n\nThe room loosened around the joke, but the missed dinner still sat with us.\n\nAang washed the rice.\n\nAfter a while he said, “When people need the Avatar, it feels wrong to think about what I want.”\n\nKatara’s hands stopped over the cutting board.\n\n“That is exactly when you have to,” she said.\n\nHe looked at her.\n\n“Wanting something doesn’t mean you choose it every time,” she continued. “But if you stop noticing what you want, duty gets to make every decision before you even arrive.”\n\nToph crunched into an apple. “And then you get weird.”\n\n“I’m already weird.”\n\n“Weirder.”\n\nI sat across from him. “What did you want last night?”\n\nHis answer took too long.\n\n“To be here.”\n\nNobody told him he should have been.\n\nThat would have turned love into another duty.\n\nInstead, I said, “Then next time, tell us that too. Even if you still go.”\n\nAang nodded.\n\nThe rice boiled over.\n\nToph pointed toward the stove. “Avatar crisis.”\n\nAang lunged for the pot while the rest of us laughed.\n\nThat evening Aang sent a messenger before leaving for another dispute. The note contained only six words: I want to stay. I’m going.\n\nKatara read it twice.\n\nToph tapped the paper and said, “Better.”\n\nI folded it into my pocket. He came home after midnight. Nobody pretended the absence had not mattered, and nobody punished him for answering a call he believed he had to answer.\n\nWe made room for both truths at once. It was harder than choosing one and calling it virtue.\n\nA month later, the pattern changed again. Aang came home early from one dispute because another mediator could finish the work. He looked almost guilty about it until Katara asked whether the town was safe. He said yes. Toph asked whether he had been necessary. He hesitated, then said no.\n\n“Congratulations,” she said. “You discovered delegation.”\n\nAang dropped into the chair beside me. “I hate how obvious you make everything sound.”\n\n“That’s because I’m wise.”\n\n“You’re twenty-one.”\n\n“Ancient.”\n\nHe laughed and stole food from my plate only after I nodded. The world had survived without every sacrifice he could possibly offer it. For one evening, he let that be good news.\n\nHe came home tired, but he came home by choice.\n","word_count":1809},"Love-And-Duty-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Festival That Wasn’t There","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Aang woke us before sunrise by ringing a bell in the courtyard.\n\nToph threw a pillow through the window.\n\nThe bell stopped.\n\nAang leaned around the doorframe five seconds later, hair windblown and expression deeply offended.\n\n“That was a ceremonial bell.”\n\n“It was a loud ceremonial bell.”\n\n“It belonged to the Eastern Air Temple.”\n\nToph sat upright in bed. “Then why was it in my courtyard?”\n\n“Our courtyard,” Katara mumbled into the pillow beside her.\n\nToph pointed toward the sound of her voice. “Traitor.”\n\nI opened one eye. “Why are we awake?”\n\nAang brightened.\n\nThat was never a good sign before dawn.\n\n“Yangchen’s Festival.”\n\nToph fell backward.\n\nKatara pushed herself up on one elbow. “Today?”\n\n“Traditionally, yes.”\n\n“You said next week.”\n\n“I was wrong.”\n\nToph’s voice came muffled through the blanket. “Avatar wisdom.”\n\nBy breakfast, the house was full of strings, cloth, prayer wheels, flour, fruit, and three Air Acolytes who had arrived carrying a crate of things Aang insisted were not decorations.\n\n“They have symbolic purpose.”\n\n“They’re ribbons,” Toph said.\n\n“They can be both.”\n\nThe festival had not been celebrated properly in more than a century.\n\nThat fact lived underneath everything Aang did that morning.\n\nHe smiled. He gave instructions. He tied colored strips of cloth from the roofline and corrected an Acolyte’s pronunciation gently enough that the young man did not notice he had been corrected at all.\n\nBut every few minutes Aang stopped.\n\nHis hand would rest on some ordinary object—a carved bowl, a length of saffron cloth, a bundle of incense—and his expression would go distant.\n\nMemory without memory.\n\nThat was one of the cruelest things about what had been taken from him. He could remember his people. He could remember individual monks, games, rules he broke, meals he hated, the way certain halls smelled when it rained.\n\nBut he had been twelve.\n\nThere were ceremonies he had watched without learning. Songs he knew only in fragments. Customs adults would have explained later, if later had happened.\n\nNow people looked at him as if being the last Air Nomad meant he must also be the complete archive of them.\n\nAt midmorning, he handed me a stack of copied festival notes.\n\n“Can you organize these?”\n\nI flipped through them. “By what?”\n\nAang frowned.\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHalf came from his own memory. Some had been copied from temple records. Others came from old Earth Kingdom travel diaries written by visitors who had watched Air Nomad festivals from outside and misunderstood half of what they saw.\n\nOne paper claimed the holiday required floating three onions down a river.\n\nAang stared at it.\n\n“I have never heard of that.”\n\nToph took the page. “Keep it.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nKatara carried trays of dough into the courtyard. “What did the festival actually celebrate?”\n\nAang’s face softened.\n\n“Gratitude. Letting go of what we don’t need. Remembering that everything changes.”\n\nToph bit into a piece of uncooked dough.\n\nKatara slapped her hand away.\n\n“That part seems useful,” I said.\n\nAang looked toward the street beyond the gate.\n\nThe city had changed even in the months I had known it. New smokestacks interrupted the horizon. Construction cranes turned above old tile roofs. People from every nation filled streets that had once belonged to one empire or another.\n\n“Yeah,” he said. “It does.”\n\nWe held the festival at the edge of town, on a low hill where an abandoned Air Nomad waystation had once stood.\n\nOnly the foundation remained.\n\nAang had found it in an old map and spent weeks getting the council to protect the site from a factory expansion. Toph said the stones were still good. Katara found a spring nearby and cleared the channel until water ran again.\n\nI made signs explaining that visitors should not climb on the ruin.\n\nNobody read them.\n\nBefore the gathering began, an elderly neighbor asked whether the ribbons were supposed to carry the names of the dead. Aang hesitated. He had imagined releasing burdens, not creating a memorial roll.\n\"I should have explained,\" he said.\n\"You explained what it means to you,\" Katara replied. \"They may need something different.\"\nToph planted a heel in the dirt. \"Also, your writing table is crooked. The left leg sank.\"\nShe lifted the ground beneath it just enough to level the brushes. Aang set blank paper beside the instructions.\n\"I can't claim this is exactly how my people celebrated,\" he told the first guests. \"Some of it I remember. Some of it we're making together. You can write, draw, or leave the paper empty.\"\nOne person wrote a brother's name. A child drew a tree.\nKatara checked where the loose ribbons would land. \"We have to collect the ones that fall into the stream. I don't want somebody else's water spoiled by our ceremony.\"\n\"I can help them rise,\" Aang said.\n\"And I can make sure they don't stay in the creek.\"\nToph brought four cups of water. \"And I can make sure you noble idiots drink something.\"\nWhen I laughed, she bumped my shoulder. The festival was imperfect. It was better than pretending history had left us flawless directions.\n\nThe gathering grew larger than expected.\n\nAir Acolytes came in gray and yellow robes. Local families arrived out of curiosity. Children chased paper gliders down the hill. Vendors appeared because vendors could smell a crowd across three districts.\n\nAang tried to object to the fried-dough cart.\n\nThen he ate two.\n\nAt noon, he stood on the old foundation and spoke.\n\nNot as the Avatar.\n\nAs Aang.\n\n“This used to be a small stop for travelers,” he said. “Not a temple. Not a sacred place because somebody important lived here. Sacred because people rested here. Shared food. Told stories. Then moved on.”\n\nThe crowd quieted.\n\nHe held one of the copied pages in his hand.\n\n“I don’t know everything about this festival.”\n\nA few Acolytes looked surprised.\n\nAang smiled a little.\n\n“I know some things. I remember some. I’ve learned others from records. And some things are gone.”\n\nThe last word landed harder than the rest.\n\nKatara moved closer to him.\n\nToph stayed where she was, feet planted on the stone.\n\nAang continued. “If we pretend I remember what I don’t, then we aren’t preserving Air Nomad culture. We’re inventing certainty because grief makes uncertainty uncomfortable.”\n\nI looked at him and felt my throat tighten.\n\n“That doesn’t mean we do nothing,” he said. “It means we practice honestly. We say what we know. We say what we don’t. We keep the values that mattered: compassion, freedom, joy, nonviolence, care for all life. And we let living people learn how to carry them forward.”\n\nThe wind rose as he spoke, tugging at ribbons overhead.\n\nFor one breath, the hill seemed crowded with ghosts.\n\nNot visible.\n\nNot supernatural.\n\nJust present in the space Aang made for them without pretending they could answer every question.\n\nLater, we tied written burdens to thin paper streamers and let them go from the ridge.\n\nNot an exact historical ritual, Aang warned everyone.\n\n“A new one,” he said.\n\nToph held her paper for a long time.\n\n“What’d you write?” I asked.\n\n“None of your business.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nShe crumpled it.\n\nThen smoothed it again.\n\nAang stood several paces away helping a child fold a glider. Katara was laughing with an elderly woman who had mistaken rice flour for face powder and somehow covered both of them in white dust.\n\nToph angled her head toward me.\n\n“You?”\n\nI looked down at my own strip.\n\nI had written: THE FEAR THAT I AM BORROWING SOMEONE ELSE’S FAMILY.\n\n“Also none of your business.”\n\n“Good answer.”\n\nWe walked to the ridge together.\n\nToph did not need to see the horizon to know where the wind was coming from. She lifted her paper and waited until the current pulled against it.\n\n“On three?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nShe let go immediately.\n\nI laughed and released mine after hers.\n\nThe papers spun upward.\n\nToph could not follow them with her eyes.\n\nI almost described it.\n\nThen I stopped myself.\n\n“Want to know?”\n\nHer mouth tilted.\n\n“Yeah.”\n\n“Yours got caught in an updraft. Mine’s losing a fight with a tree.”\n\n“Sounds right.”\n\nAang came up behind us.\n\n“Toph, did you actually write something?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re a terrible liar.”\n\n“I’m the best liar. I can tell when everybody else is doing it.”\n\n“That is not how that works.”\n\nKatara joined us and slipped an arm around my waist.\n\n“What did you write?” I asked her.\n\nShe looked toward the streamers.\n\n“That I don’t have to keep every version of home exactly the way I remember it.”\n\nAang turned toward her.\n\nKatara gave him a small smile.\n\n“The South is changing. This city is changing. We’re changing.”\n\nToph made a gagging noise.\n\nKatara leaned over and kissed her cheek.\n\nToph froze.\n\nThen, very deliberately, wiped her cheek on my shoulder.\n\n“Gross.”\n\n“You’re blushing,” Aang said.\n\n“I will bury you in this sacred historical site.”\n\n“You can’t. Paperwork put up a sign.”\n\nI pointed downhill. “Finally, respect for municipal authority.”\n\nThat evening, after the crowd left, we stayed to clean.\n\nThe hill was littered with cups, snapped strings, crumbs, and the evidence of three hundred people attempting spirituality near food vendors.\n\nAang swept with a broom instead of bending.\n\nI asked why.\n\nHe shrugged. “Monks made us clean after festivals.”\n\n“Tradition?”\n\n“Punishment.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Being children near a festival.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe leaned on the broom.\n\n“Was it wrong?” he asked.\n\nI knew what he meant.\n\nThe new ritual. The things he had filled in. The celebration built partly from memory and partly from choice.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“No,” I said. “I know you told the truth about what you knew. You didn’t ask anyone to confuse invention with history.”\n\nHis gaze dropped.\n\n“And it made people care enough to learn more.”\n\nAang looked across the hill where an Air Acolyte carefully wrapped an old stone marker before carrying it indoors for preservation.\n\nThe wind moved through his robes.\n\n“Maybe that’s enough for now.”\n\n“Maybe ‘for now’ is allowed.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen he hooked the broom behind my knees with airbending and almost knocked me into a basket.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“Sacred tradition?”\n\n“Extremely.”\n\nI chased the Avatar down the hill while Toph shouted unhelpful tactical advice and Katara laughed hard enough to drop the trash bag.\n\nBehind us, new ribbons snapped in the evening wind above old stones.\n\nNot restoration.\n\nNot replacement.\n\nSomething living.\n","word_count":1736},"Love-And-Duty-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Vote","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"We lost by one vote.\n\nAfter months of hearings, petitions, neighborhood meetings, revised language, public arguments, private arguments, and one memorable session where Toph threatened to bend a councilman’s chair into a smaller chair, the Civic Balance Charter failed seven to six.\n\nThe chamber erupted.\n\nSupporters shouted.\n\nOpponents cheered.\n\nMara Sen said a word that caused three reporters to stop pretending not to listen.\n\nI sat at the clerk’s table and stared at the tally.\n\nSeven.\n\nSix.\n\nOne vote.\n\nThe charter would have created enforceable anti-discrimination rules for city employment and housing, independent safety standards for bending and nonbending workers, public conflict-of-interest logs, and elected neighborhood councils with reserved nonbender representation where bending professions dominated local government.\n\nIt was not perfect.\n\nThat was part of why losing hurt.\n\nWe had compromised until every sentence had fingerprints on it.\n\nThe deciding vote belonged to Councilor Chen.\n\nOf course it did.\n\nHe had spent six weeks describing himself as undecided and two days before the vote received a large campaign donation from an industrial association that opposed the worker-safety provisions.\n\nLegal.\n\nTechnically.\n\nI hated technically.\n\nAfter the chamber emptied, a textile worker named Suri approached the clerk's table. Her hands were stained indigo, and she'd worn the same patched coat to every hearing. She had argued for the nonbender safety provisions so consistently that I recognized the scrape of her shoes on the tile before she spoke.\n\n“I want a copy of the roll call,” she said.\n\n“The votes are public,” the clerk answered.\n\n“Not the speeches. The votes. I want my neighbors to know who actually stood up for the rule about fireproof exits.”\n\nI pushed the tally sheet toward her. Her fingertip rested on Chen's name for a moment, then moved down the column.\n\n“People will tell us we lost because we asked for too much,” she said. “I thought it wasn't too much to want to reach the door before a furnace explodes.”\n\n“It isn't.”\n\n“Then don't come back with a version where we have to give that up just to get six votes.”\n\nI had been drafting concessions in my head since the result was called. The pages in my folder seemed to grow heavier.\n\n“You might not like our next proposal,” I said.\n\n“I might not. You're supposed to bring it to us before you ask me to sell it to the workshop.”\n\nThat hurt more than Chen's smug smile had. She was right. I'd started thinking of my neighbors as a constituency I needed to reassure instead of adults who could disagree with the strategy.\n\nI wrote down her name and the workshop's requested meeting time, then stopped myself.\n\n“May I give the committee your contact?”\n\n“No. Tell them the union steward will send a representative. I've got a shift tomorrow.”\n\nShe took her copy of the votes and went. I sat with the ink drying under my hand, thinking of every time I'd told Aang he didn't get to decide the future for other people. Apparently the lesson was not reserved for the Avatar.\n\nAang stood in the public gallery.\n\nHe had refused to use Avatar authority to pressure the council.\n\nThat was correct.\n\nI hated that too.\n\nKatara sat beside him, jaw tight.\n\nToph was not allowed in the chamber after the chair incident.\n\nShe waited outside.\n\nThe council adjourned.\n\nPeople poured into the hall.\n\nReporters surrounded Aang immediately.\n\n“Avatar, will you override the vote?”\n\n“Do you believe the council has failed nonbenders?”\n\n“Will you withdraw support from Councilor Chen?”\n\nAang’s face went still in the public way.\n\n“No,” he said. “The charter was a civil legislative process. I supported it publicly, but I will not turn disagreement into a reason for the Avatar to replace elected government.”\n\nA reporter pushed closer. “Even when the result harms balance?”\n\nAang’s expression sharpened.\n\n“Balance is not me getting every outcome I want.”\n\nI heard the sentence.\n\nI understood it.\n\nI wanted to scream.\n\nMara caught my arm before I walked away.\n\n“Don’t disappear.”\n\n“I’m going outside.”\n\n“You’re going home to write a better charter at two in the morning.”\n\n“That is slander.”\n\n“It’s prophecy.”\n\n“I’m not the Avatar.”\n\n“Thank every spirit available.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nThen I nearly cried.\n\nMara’s grip softened.\n\n“Losing is part of politics.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“There you go knowing things again.”\n\nI glared.\n\nShe released me.\n\n“Come to the meeting tomorrow.”\n\n“What meeting?”\n\n“The one where we decide what we do next.”\n\nI stared at her.\n\n“You scheduled it already?”\n\n“I expected either victory problems or defeat problems.”\n\nI loved her a little.\n\nOutside, Toph leaned against the courthouse wall.\n\n“Well?”\n\nI did not need to answer.\n\nShe could hear the crowd.\n\n“Seven-six,” I said.\n\nToph’s mouth flattened.\n\n“Chen?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Chair.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Tiny chair.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Stool?”\n\n“Toph.”\n\nShe kicked the wall.\n\nA small crack appeared.\n\n“Property damage,” I said automatically.\n\n“Put it on my tab.”\n\nKatara emerged next.\n\nShe looked furious enough to freeze the rain.\n\nAang followed, surrounded by aides.\n\nOne handed him another schedule.\n\nAang looked at it.\n\nThen tore it in half.\n\nEveryone stopped.\n\nThe aide stared.\n\nAang said, “I’m going home.”\n\nIt was a tiny rebellion.\n\nI loved it.\n\nAt home, nobody spoke for the first ten minutes.\n\nToph opened the bottle she had bought for the housing hearing months ago.\n\nKatara made tea because she said we were not drinking on empty stomachs.\n\nAang sat on the kitchen floor with his back against a cabinet.\n\nI put the vote tally on the table.\n\nSeven.\n\nSix.\n\nToph tapped the paper.\n\n“So we change one.”\n\nKatara sat across from me. “Or the language.”\n\nAang rubbed both hands over his face.\n\n“Or the public.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe lowered his hands.\n\n“Chen can take money because voters don’t punish him for it.”\n\n“That’s cynical,” Katara said.\n\n“It’s also true,” I said.\n\nAang sighed.\n\n“I’m trying to be less naive.”\n\nToph pointed at him. “Don’t overcorrect. You’ll get boring.”\n\nHe threw a towel at her.\n\nShe caught it.\n\nI stared at the tally.\n\n“I thought if the argument was good enough…”\n\nMara’s voice echoed in my head.\n\nLosing is part of politics.\n\nKatara said, “Arguments matter. So does power.”\n\nAang looked at her.\n\nShe continued. “Who has money. Who has time to attend meetings. Who gets believed. Who is afraid of losing work if they sign a petition.”\n\nToph added, “Who owns the building the meeting happens in.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\n“That was almost profound.”\n\n“Shut up.”\n\nWe spent the evening not solving it.\n\nThat was harder than solving things.\n\nAang kept reaching for possibilities and putting them back down because each one crossed some line of influence he did not want to cross.\n\nKatara wanted accountability immediately and hated that due process was slow.\n\nToph wanted direct pressure and hated any system that rewarded politeness from people with less power.\n\nI wanted to rewrite the text until language could defeat money.\n\nNone of us got what we wanted.\n\nAround midnight, I stood to get my folders.\n\nThree voices said, “No.”\n\nI stopped.\n\n“This is different.”\n\nToph threw the towel again.\n\n“It’s midnight, Paperwork.”\n\n“We just lost.”\n\n“Yeah,” Katara said. “We lost.”\n\nShe came around the table.\n\n“And you are not going to turn losing into punishment.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\nAang’s expression softened.\n\n“No penance by paperwork.”\n\n“That phrase is offensive.”\n\n“It’s good,” Toph said.\n\n“Betrayal.”\n\nKatara took my hand.\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\nI did.\n\nBadly.\n\nAt three in the morning, I woke and found Aang sitting by the window.\n\n“So much for rest,” I whispered.\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n“I was trying.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nThe city outside was dark except for factory lamps and a few late windows.\n\n“Do you regret not intervening?” I asked.\n\nAang took a long time to answer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThat surprised me.\n\n“And no.”\n\n“Very helpful.”\n\n“I could have pressured Chen.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“People would probably praise me for it.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And then the next Avatar might pressure a council for something worse.”\n\nI leaned my head against the wall.\n\n“Duty is annoying.”\n\nAang laughed quietly.\n\n“I thought you liked rules.”\n\n“I like rules other people follow.”\n\nToph’s voice came from the hallway.\n\n“I heard that.”\n\nWe both jumped.\n\nShe walked in carrying a blanket.\n\nKatara followed with three more.\n\n“You’re all terrible at sleeping,” she said.\n\n“We were having a constitutional discussion,” Aang said.\n\n“At three in the morning.”\n\n“Democracy never rests.”\n\nToph groaned. “Kill it.”\n\nKatara draped a blanket over all of us.\n\nWe sat by the window until dawn.\n\nWe had lost.\n\nNo hidden victory.\n\nNo miraculous reversal.\n\nJust one vote and the work after it.\n\nWhen sunlight reached the factory roofs, I finally understood why the defeat mattered to the story we were building.\n\nLove did not guarantee justice.\n\nGood arguments did not guarantee votes.\n\nPower used carefully was still power.\n\nDuty sometimes meant refusing the shortcut even when the shortcut led somewhere you desperately wanted to go.\n\nAt nine, I went to Mara’s meeting.\n\nI left my pen at home on purpose.\n\nToph brought me another one.\n\nMara’s meeting took place in a bakery after closing.\n\nNobody had reserved a hall. Nobody had an agenda printed on official paper. Someone wrote discussion points on the back of flour invoices and passed them around with leftover rolls.\n\nI listened.\n\nThat was harder than speaking.\n\nA machinist wanted the charter split into smaller ordinances so opponents could not kill every reform with one vote. A teacher argued that doing so would let the council approve symbolic pieces while avoiding the parts that redistributed actual authority. Two shop owners disagreed about whether another public hearing would help or merely repeat the same speeches.\n\nNo one asked me to solve it.\n\nEventually Mara looked over. “You’ve been quiet.”\n\n“I’m practicing.”\n\n“Painful?”\n\n“Excruciating.”\n\nThe room laughed.\n\nI unfolded my hands. “The council knows how to defeat our current proposal. So we stop giving them the same proposal.”\n\nThat started another hour of argument.\n\nGood.\n\nWhen I came home, Toph was asleep on the couch with a pen tucked behind one ear. Aang had fallen asleep on the floor beside a stack of cultural-school applications. Katara was still awake, reading by the stove.\n\n“How was it?” she asked.\n\n“Messy.”\n\nHer smile said she understood.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n“We might lose again.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That doesn’t make tonight pointless?”\n\n“No.”\n\nI leaned my head against her shoulder.\n\nAcross the room, Toph muttered without opening her eyes, “Told you.”\n\n“You were asleep.”\n\n“Still right.”\n\nThe first vote had taught me something ugly and useful: losing did not automatically mean the work had failed. Sometimes it meant the strategy had.\n\nThe next morning, my borrowed pen was still on the table. I returned it to Toph.\n\nShe pushed it back.\n\n“Keep it.”\n\n“For luck?”\n\n“Don’t be ridiculous.”\n\nI kept it anyway.\n\nI used that pen at the next meeting. We still argued. This time, losing was no longer the only ending I could imagine.\n","word_count":1828},"love-and-duty-42":{"number":42,"title":"The Shape of Fair Time","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"Our family scheduling argument began with a square drawn in blue ink.\n\nIt was supposed to represent Tuesday.\n\nKatara had divided the page into days, with each day divided into morning, afternoon, and evening. She had written clinic shifts in neat columns. Aang's council meetings occupied the top half of the week. Toph's bridge inspections ran along the bottom in a handwriting large enough to be considered structural reinforcement.\n\nMy own commitments were squeezed into the margins.\n\n\"That's not Tuesday,\" I said.\n\nKatara examined the square.\n\n\"It is literally Tuesday.\"\n\n\"It looks like a punishment.\"\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Give me the calendar. I'll earthbend us a new week.\"\n\n\"We cannot bend time,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Then we stop pretending Tuesday can hold four people and the whole city,\" I said.\nKatara tapped the clinic column. \"These are patients. They don't disappear because I have a date.\"\n\"Nor should they. But you can ask which shifts someone assumed you'd cover for free.\"\nToph jabbed her finger at the bridge inspection. \"Mine happens in daylight. Bad bolts don't schedule around romance.\"\nAang reread a council request. \"They want me to settle the crossing dispute.\"\n\"Who asked? The neighbors or the secretary?\" I said.\nHe studied the signature. \"The secretary.\"\n\"There's the Avatar-shaped hole everybody throws work into,\" Toph said.\nAang wrote a question instead of an acceptance. Katara agreed to ask another qualified healer whether they wanted a paid shift. I put my Starwake errands in their own column and reminded everyone that my preference for polyamory did not turn every hour of my life into shared property.\nToph tipped back her chair. \"Fine. Now we just need someone to invent a second Tuesday.\"\nAang brightened. \"I could—\"\n\"No,\" Katara and I said together.\nFor once he listened before trying to make the world more convenient.\n\n\"Then why are you the Avatar?\"\n\nHe gave her the patient look he used when he was trying very hard to remember that he loved her.\n\nI had agreed to the meeting because the previous week had been a mess. I was supposed to help Iroh at the pantry on Wednesday, check the supply ledger at Brasswatch on Thursday, and spend Friday evening with Katara, just the two of us. Instead, the bridge inspection ran late, Danse needed an additional sample, and I forgot to tell Katara that I'd promised to review a different set of measurements.\n\nBy the time I arrived home Friday, there were two cold bowls on the table and no one in the kitchen.\n\nKatara hadn't shouted.\n\nThat was worse.\n\nNow she sat across from me with her hands folded, waiting.\n\n\"Tell me what bothered you,\" I said.\n\n\"I waited,\" she answered. \"Then I stopped waiting because I had an early shift. I don't mind that you help people. I mind finding out you won't be home from a note you leave after I've already made plans.\"\n\nI stared at the calendar.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nAang looked relieved.\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"That was fast. Usually people spend an hour defending themselves first.\"\n\n\"I can still do that if you want entertainment.\"\n\n\"No thanks.\"\n\nKatara looked at me, not smiling.\n\n\"I know our relationships are complicated. I know we agreed we weren't going to measure affection by equal hours. But when we make plans, I need to be able to trust them.\"\n\n\"I agree.\"\n\nAang cleared his throat.\n\n\"I think I've done something similar.\"\n\nToph turned toward him.\n\n\"Only the part where you promise you'll be home by sundown and then spend three hours negotiating with a council because you don't want anybody upset.\"\n\nAang winced.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\n\"You're all painfully reasonable today.\"\n\n\"Give us time,\" I said.\n\nThe difficulty wasn't that we had too many people to love. It was that we kept mistaking good intentions for a working system. Nobody wanted to appear selfish. So we waited until the disappointment hurt enough to become an argument.\n\nKatara pushed the blue square toward me.\n\n\"Tell me where you actually need time this week.\"\n\n\"Monday evening with you, if you want it. Wednesday I need a quiet night by myself. Thursday I'm with Iroh for the pantry inventory. Saturday I promised Greg I'd help set up the pier.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"And when do we spend time together?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Do you mean everyone, or you and me?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I meant everyone. But maybe I want both.\"\n\n\"Then we should write both.\"\n\nToph tapped the edge of the table.\n\n\"And stop treating the whole group as one giant person.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" Katara said.\n\nToph leaned back, pleased with herself.\n\nI wrote separate lines for individual time and group time. It looked less tidy. It was more honest.\n\nWe found out quickly that fairness was not the same as giving everyone an identical share of every week. Katara needed reliable advance notice because of her clinic shifts. Aang wanted space for spontaneous visits without making them obligatory. Toph preferred a whole afternoon of work and teasing to an evening of ceremonious romance. I needed time alone and time for other people I cared about. None of those needs were defects.\n\nThen Toph made a noise like she'd swallowed a nail.\n\n\"What?\" I asked.\n\n\"You've left a space for me on Friday.\"\n\n\"Do you want it?\"\n\n\"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Then it stays open. No obligation.\"\n\nShe turned her head toward me.\n\n\"You don't have to keep a chair for someone who hasn't said yes.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to offer.\"\n\nFor a moment she looked almost shy.\n\n\"Maybe,\" she said.\n\nI nearly fell off my chair.\n\nAang smiled into his tea.\n\nKatara caught him.\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm not doing anything.\"\n\n\"You're glowing.\"\n\n\"Airbender condition.\"\n\nToph threw a napkin at him.\n\nThe calendar looked tidy enough to be suspicious.\n\nBefore I could congratulate us, Toph tapped the corner of the page.\n\n“That's not a calendar,” she said. “That's a trap with very tidy boxes.”\n\nKatara frowned. “We've left free time.”\n\n“Free for whom? My bridge review starts at dawn. If I spend Tuesday night with you, I get four hours of sleep and then somebody asks me why the west supports are crooked.”\n\nAang looked at the row he'd marked for himself. He had set aside three consecutive evenings for council negotiations. He hadn't included the time he'd need to prepare.\n\n“I thought I could finish early,” he said.\n\nToph made a derisive noise. “I thought I could teach the entire city to earthbend by lunchtime. We're both disappointed.”\n\nI moved my own commitments out of the margin and onto a separate sheet. One belonged to Greg in Beach City, another to Danse at Brasswatch, another to a quiet evening I wanted entirely alone. The fact that Jasper was the person who traveled between the doors did not make the whole set of obligations flexible.\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous,” I told them, “and I prefer polyamory. You know that. But a calendar doesn't turn all the people I love into one giant relationship with interchangeable hours.”\n\nKatara nodded. “And being together doesn't mean we stop needing time apart.”\n\nAang took the brush. “Then we should put *rest* on the page first.”\n\n“And no assigning *my* rest,” Toph added.\n\nShe drummed her heel against the floorboards while I marked my writing time. I was surprised at how relieved I felt seeing an empty square stay empty. Nobody filled it with a favor.\n\nWhen the final version was ready, we agreed to try it for a week. A schedule could be an invitation to keep promises; it was not permission to make them for someone else.\n\nI counted the squares once more, then realized I'd put three people's needs on Wednesday evening and left Thursday almost empty. It had seemed fair when I was filling in boxes. It looked less fair when I imagined actual people trying to get through a door.\n\n\"Can we test one week before calling this finished?\" I asked.\n\nToph leaned back. \"The schedule has homework now?\"\n\n\"It always did,\" Katara said.\n\nAang agreed to handle Appa's morning care on the days Katara had clinic appointments. Toph wanted the bridge review kept separate from household chores, because having a gift for stone didn't mean she was available to fix every broken shelf. I wrote down my writing time and asked everyone not to treat it as an empty square simply because I would be home.\n\n\"How do we tell when somebody's overwhelmed?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Ask them,\" Toph said. \"Revolutionary concept.\"\n\nKatara laughed. \"And believe the answer.\"\n\nWe tried a mock change: a clinic message arriving during a planned family dinner. Katara would decide whether she needed to go. Aang would check whether transport was required without volunteering Appa before discussing the return. Toph would not be drafted to babysit anyone's emotional reaction, and I would be allowed to say I was disappointed without making the disappointment an argument.\n\nThe first attempt was awful. We all talked at once.\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said, \"we should practice one person speaking at a time.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Not a chance.\"\n\nBut she waited for me to finish. Aang went next, then Katara. The conversation took longer, and everyone understood more of it.\n\nI thought about how long it had taken us to name the relationship as polyamorous, about telling each partner plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred love that could be shared honestly. A calendar couldn't do the loving for us. It could stop ordinary mistakes from becoming proof that somebody was unwanted.\n\nBy supper, the plan was imperfect but usable. Katara leaned against my shoulder while Aang cleared the table. Toph stole the last dumpling and claimed it was part of the test.\n\n\"Of what?\" I asked.\n\n\"Whether you can survive disappointment.\"\n\n\"Apparently I can.\"\n\nShe smiled in the particular way that warned me she was pleased, then kicked my chair gently under the table. It was nothing like a formal vow. It felt more like a family.\n\nWe finished the calendar in an hour, which was astonishing considering how often Toph tried to rename Wednesday. We made one rule I cared about particularly: changing a plan meant actually telling the people affected. No silent assumptions that a loving partner would understand without being asked.\n\nThen we discovered the missing problem.\n\nThe house had only one quiet room.\n\nAang used it to meditate. Katara used it to prepare clinic records. Toph went there when too much noise made it hard to concentrate on what she felt through the floor. I went there to write.\n\nAll four of us had been politely avoiding it when someone else was inside.\n\n\"Which means,\" I said, \"we need to talk about the room as seriously as the relationship.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally. A structural problem.\"\n\nWe spent the afternoon moving furniture. Aang made a low screen from cloth and light wood. Katara rearranged the shelves so medicines stayed in the proper light. Toph checked that the chairs could be moved without blocking the door. I measured the table twice and still got one dimension wrong.\n\nToph corrected me.\n\n\"You're terrible at lying to a ruler.\"\n\n\"I didn't lie.\"\n\n\"Your ruler did.\"\n\nBy evening the room had two workable areas and a sign that could be turned to say PLEASE KNOCK. It did not solve every issue, but it meant needing privacy didn't require giving a speech.\n\nKatara found me there after dinner.\n\nShe tapped on the frame.\n\n\"Can I come in?\"\n\nI turned the sign over.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nShe entered carrying two cups of tea and a small plate of dumplings. She set them down, then stood beside the table.\n\n\"I was still angry this morning,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm less angry now.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not because we drew squares.\"\n\n\"That was my favorite part.\"\n\nShe laughed softly.\n\n\"Because you listened. And because you didn't promise you'll never disappoint me again.\"\n\n\"I probably will.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\nI stood. Her hands were warm when I took them.\n\n\"I'm sorry I left you waiting,\" I said. \"I will call or send word before the plan changes next time. Not afterward.\"\n\nShe stepped closer.\n\n\"That's what I needed.\"\n\nThe kiss was quiet at first. She brushed her thumb against my cheek, and I felt the pull of affection and embarrassment together, the strange tenderness of being forgiven without being excused. When I leaned in again, she smiled against my mouth.\n\n\"Monday,\" she murmured.\n\n\"Monday.\"\n\n\"Don't make me put it on a banner.\"\n\n\"Toph would.\"\n\n\"She would spell it wrong on purpose.\"\n\nWe laughed and stayed together until the tea cooled.\n\nThe next morning, Aang added an extra square to the calendar.\n\nIt read NO SCHEDULING DURING BREAKFAST.\n\nToph changed it to NO MEETINGS BEFORE DUMPLINGS.\n\nNobody objected.\n\nIt was the first schedule we'd made that felt less like a test and more like a way to give our choices somewhere to live.","word_count":2145},"love-and-duty-48":{"number":48,"title":"Water for the Other Side","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The spreadsheet said we had enough water.\n\nThe people waiting at the east end of the bridge said otherwise.\n\nI stared at the figures and felt my face grow warm. The table I'd made showed thirty containers delivered to Riverstone Terrace, twelve to the clinic, eight to Brasswatch Yard, and six to the temporary shelter on the ridge. The total matched the supply account.\n\nIt was beautiful arithmetic.\n\nIt was a terrible distribution.\n\nKatara stood beside me, reading the paper.\n\n\"How many people are at the east shelter?\" she asked.\n\n\"Eighteen.\"\n\n\"And at the west?\"\n\n\"Seven.\"\n\n\"How many containers?\"\n\n\"Six east. Ten west.\"\n\nShe tapped the page.\n\n\"There's our problem.\"\n\nThe bridge had remained closed while the supports were repaired and tested after the storm. People could use the longer ridge route, but not every family could carry heavy containers that distance. The supply team had delivered water to the places closest to the storage depot first, then sent what remained to the harder-to-reach shelter.\n\nNobody had set out to be cruel.\n\nThe process had been convenient for the people carrying the crates, not fair to those who needed them.\n\nI reached for my pencil.\n\n\"We can fix the numbers.\"\n\n\"Eventually,\" Katara said. \"But first we need to talk to the people who are short.\"\n\nWe walked to the east shelter with Aang. The wind was still strong along the canal, and the lower path smelled of wet stone. A temporary ramp led into the hall, built after Edda's access inspection.\n\nA woman named Sela greeted us at the door.\n\n\"Do you have water?\"\n\n\"Some,\" Katara said. \"Not enough yet. We're checking what everyone needs.\"\n\nSela pointed to a row of empty vessels.\n\n\"Some of us can drink from the spring if we carry it. Others can't carry the distance. The oldest person here needs water brought to her, and the family with the baby is boiling what they can find.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Then they go first.\"\n\nI wrote down the specific needs without putting private names on the public board.\n\nAang looked at the route.\n\n\"I could carry water across the canal with bending.\"\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"You could. What happens when you leave?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"We need a system that doesn't depend on me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI felt a small rush of admiration. He'd learned to hear that question without treating it as an insult.\n\nWe returned to the square and called the distribution volunteers together. Mara had been packing supplies, Danse had helped design the carrying frames, and Greg had offered a morning shift with the handcart. Toph stood near the wall, listening to the movement through the stone floor.\n\nI put the numbers on the board.\n\n\"These are the deliveries,\" I said. \"But this is not the same as the needs.\"\n\nOne volunteer frowned.\n\n\"We delivered to every district.\"\n\n\"Yes. We didn't deliver proportionately to the people who couldn't access the other supplies.\"\n\nAnother looked embarrassed.\n\n\"We used the closest route first.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why we're changing it.\"\n\nToph tapped the floor.\n\n\"Your chart counts containers. It doesn't count the distance a person has to drag them.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nWe made a new plan with actual requirements. Katara confirmed the clinic's medical supply needs. Sela arranged a contact person at the east shelter without making the residents' private details public. Danse checked the handcart load. Edda tested the route so the deliveries wouldn't block the accessible path.\n\nAster came from Brasswatch carrying a spare lamp and a note with a corner torn away. They had been checking the backup lights near the workshop when somebody found water pooling beside the service junction.\n\n“Renn says the generator may have to stay down tonight,” Aster told us. “We need the drinking-water cart to use the north entrance. The service corridor's being kept clear for electrical inspection.”\n\nI looked at my chart. The fastest route to the east shelter ran past the very doorway Aster had named. A quicker path for water would now send a heavy cart through a place where someone could be working on wet cables.\n\n“Can the shelter wait until morning?” I asked.\n\nAmel, who had walked over with the latest request sheet, gave me a look that made my ears burn.\n\n“No. People are drinking the last of their reserve tonight.”\n\nToph tapped the paving with her heel. “Then find another route. The water isn't going to sprout legs because your chart says it's inconvenient.”\n\nAang volunteered to carry the first two containers himself. Katara stopped him long enough to ask whether somebody could do the rest without the Avatar. Beren, who was loading the cart, drew the wider path on the back of my old delivery sheet. Edda walked it with him, checked the turns, and marked where a loose crate would narrow the entrance.\n\nAster folded the new route into their pocket to show Danse before the lights failed altogether.\n\nIt meant the water would arrive later, but by a path we had actually tested. I had spent so much time making the first numbers look balanced that I had almost delivered water into an electrical hazard.\n\nWhen I crossed out the old line, the ink ran through two entire columns. I didn't bother making it neat. I wrote the new arrival time in the space below and asked Amel to read it back to me.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers for the first round, with two local volunteers learning the handling process for later shifts.\n\nNo one was assigned more than they'd agreed to do.\n\nThe revised plan meant the west shelter would receive fewer extra containers for one day. Some residents complained. They had been relieved to have a reserve and did not like hearing that it had been allocated unevenly.\n\nI couldn't blame them for feeling anxious.\n\nI could still tell the truth.\n\n\"We're not taking away your minimum needs,\" I explained. \"We're correcting a distribution that left another group without theirs.\"\n\nA man near the depot folded his arms.\n\n\"Easy for you to say.\"\n\nHe was right about one thing: I was not the person who would have to walk farther for water if the plan failed.\n\n\"Then help us test it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"We need your experience of the route. And if you find a problem, I want to know.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nWe took the draft to the east shelter before asking anyone to call it fair. A woman named Amel was distributing cups near the entrance. She held the sheet with two fingers, as though it might leave ink on her hands.\n\n\"Thirty containers,\" she read. \"For the whole terrace?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How many of those are supposed to arrive before the night shift starts?\"\n\nI checked. \"Twelve.\"\n\nShe tapped a line with her nail. \"We have six people working outside until dark. They won't be here to collect water at that hour.\"\n\nI was ready to explain the schedule, then stopped. The schedule was mine. The people waiting for water hadn't agreed to live according to it.\n\n\"What time would actually work?\" I asked.\n\nShe wrote two possible windows in the margin, then asked whether the depot could hold a few containers for people who preferred not to explain their household arrangements in public. Katara said the shelter could record quantities without publishing names. That was different from hiding how much water it had received.\n\nAang wanted to help with deliveries. Amel looked at his glider.\n\n\"Not everything can arrive from the sky,\" she said. \"Some people need to know what happens when you're not here.\"\n\nHe winced, then nodded. \"You're right.\"\n\nToph tested the stone lip of the north path with her foot. \"And the cart still has to turn a corner. Which brilliant person drew that?\"\n\nI raised my hand.\n\n\"Of course you did.\"\n\nI laughed despite the heat in my cheeks. She wasn't gentle about an error, but she didn't make me afraid to change it. There was affection in her bluntness, and I wasn't required to enjoy it every minute to know she cared.\n\nWhen the sheet finally had a plan that Amel could use, I passed her the pen. She signed the receipt for the proposed time, not for water that had yet to arrive.\n\n\"Come back when the carts do,\" she said.\n\nWe did. The plan still needed a driver's eye before it could become a real route.\n\nThe first revision nearly failed at the depot.\n\nA cart driver named Beren looked at the new sheet and shook his head.\n\n\"You've moved the east delivery earlier, but you're still using the narrow lane by the canal. My cart can't turn around there when the rain comes.\"\n\nI studied the map. He was right. In my hurry to fix the unequal water totals, I had reused the old route without checking whether the carriers could actually make the journey.\n\n\"Which path would you use?\" I asked.\n\n\"The north path. Longer, but the stones are solid.\"\n\nToph tapped her foot against the ground.\n\n\"He's right about the surface.\"\n\n\"Can Edda check the turns?\" Katara asked.\n\n\"Already did yesterday,\" Beren said. \"I watched her. She made me move a stack of crates so she could pass.\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Thank you for doing that.\"\n\n\"She didn't ask politely.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Sounds like Edda.\"\n\nWe changed the route.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers along the first run, but Sela reminded him that the local volunteers needed practice managing the handcart without bending. They divided the loads by what people could safely handle. Aang worked with them, not instead of them.\n\nAt the east shelter, a woman asked whether the delivery record would name the people who received extra water.\n\n\"No,\" Katara said. \"The public record will show how much arrived and what needs it served. Your medical or family details aren't for the notice board.\"\n\nThe woman nodded and accepted the containers.\n\nI crossed out another line on the spreadsheet. The new table looked worse than the old one. It had arrows, corrections, and a reminder to review the distribution after two days.\n\nIt was also the first version that people who used the routes had helped write.\n\nI had spent so long worrying about being a responsible member of our complicated household that I'd forgotten the same principle mattered outside our door. Caring for people wasn't making decisions for them. It was asking which decisions actually changed the day they had to live.\n\nThe second delivery went better. The east shelter received its immediate supplies before evening. The west retained enough for confirmed needs. The public ledger showed the changes and the reasons, without turning vulnerable households into a list of people to be judged.\n\nWe returned home exhausted.\n\nKatara washed the dust from her hands and looked at my spreadsheet, now covered in corrections.\n\n\"Do you still like it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. That means it's being used.\"\n\nToph sat at the table with a bowl of soup.\n\n\"Spreadsheets are like people,\" she announced. \"They get annoying when they think they're always right.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n\"Are you comparing me to a spreadsheet?\"\n\n\"Only on your worst days.\"\n\nKatara came around the table and rested a hand on my shoulder.\n\n\"You noticed a problem,\" she said. \"Then you listened. That's what matters.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"We still missed it for two days.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe didn't soften the truth.\n\nThen she kissed my forehead.\n\n\"We'll do better tomorrow.\"\n\nLater, when the dishes were done, Aang showed me the new route he'd drawn. It included shaded rest points, safe crossing times, and notes from the people who used the path daily. His handwriting was crooked from trying to fit too much information onto one page.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"It looks terrible.\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"That might be the new standard.\"\n\nHe grinned and leaned close.\n\n\"Then perhaps I can ask for something that doesn't fit in a table.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"A quiet walk with you.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\nWe went outside beneath the lanterns.\n\nThe bridge remained closed. The water was still moving. The arithmetic was still imperfect.\n\nBut the people on the other side had water, and we'd stopped confusing a balanced column with a fair day.","word_count":2050}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="love-and-duty");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=83842;})();
